Very interesting! The commit `1539be4` (Unify individual toggle to single click handler with preventDefault) had:

In the previous version (shown as `-`):
```javascript
if (!target.classList.contains('toggle')) return;
var li = target.closest('li');
if (!li) return;
var id = parseInt(li.getAttribute('data-id'), 10);
var todos = loadTodos();
var todo = todos.find(function (t) { return t.id === id; });
if (todo) {
  todo.completed = target.checked;  // <--- USED target.checked
  saveTodos(todos);
  li.classList.toggle('completed', todo.completed);
  updateTodoCount(todos);
  updateClearCompleted(todos);
  updateToggleAll(todos);
  // Remove li from DOM when it no longer matches the active filter
  var currentFilter = getCurrentFilter();
  if (currentFilter === '#/active' && todo.completed) {
    li.remove();
  } else if (currentFilter === '#/completed' && !todo.completed) {
    li.remove();
  }
}
```

Wait, this was a `change` event listener! The previous version (before 1539be4) used a `change` event listener on `todoList` for individual toggles! It used `target.checked` directly.

Then commit `1539be4` changed it to a `click` listener with `preventDefault()`, setting `target.checked` explicitly.

Then `e0d0a6c` added `preventDefault()` to the click handler that was already using `e.preventDefault()`.

Then the subsequent commits removed `preventDefault()` and tried different approaches.

So the `change` event approach was TRIED BEFORE and it was the basis for commit 1539be4's predecessor. But that approach was changed because it failed with... let me look at the commit message.

